consiSt("ks",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Kansas Supreme Court","d":"At least seven justices; every case is heard by at least four, and a decision needs a majority of those sitting and at least four votes. Original jurisdiction in quo warranto, mandamus, and habeas corpus; appellate jurisdiction as provided by law, including direct appeals when a statute is held unconstitutional and discretionary review of Court of Appeals decisions. It has general administrative authority over all Kansas courts and makes the rules for bar admission and lawyer discipline. Justices are appointed by the Governor from three nominees of the Supreme Court Nominating Commission and then stand for retention (Kan. Const. art. 3, §§ 1-3, 5; K.S.A. 7-103; 60-2101(b))."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Kansas Court of Appeals","d":"Fourteen judges (K.S.A. 20-3002), appointed under K.S.A. 20-3020. Hears most civil appeals from the district courts and some agency appeals; cases may be transferred to the Supreme Court on motion within 30 days or on certification (K.S.A. 20-3001, 20-3016 to 20-3018). The Clerk of the Supreme Court is also its clerk (K.S.A. 20-3015)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts","d":"A district court sits in each county with general original jurisdiction over civil and criminal matters (K.S.A. 20-301), organized into 31 judicial districts (K.S.A. 4-202 through 4-232). District judges serve four-year terms and are either elected or chosen by nonpartisan merit selection depending on the district’s vote (Kan. Const. art. 3, § 6). Civil procedure is statutory: the Code of Civil Procedure, K.S.A. chapter 60."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"District magistrate judges; limited actions","d":"District magistrate judges sit within the district court. Absent consent, they may not hear civil cases over $10,000 (other than unsecured contract debt for goods, services, or money), injunctions, class actions, declaratory judgments, real-estate title or foreclosure, and other listed matters. Appeals from a magistrate who is not a lawyer go to a district judge within 14 days (K.S.A. 20-302b; 60-2103a). Smaller civil cases may proceed under the Code of Civil Procedure for Limited Actions, K.S.A. chapter 61 (not collected), which article 2 of chapter 60 does not govern (K.S.A. 60-201(b))."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Public Speech Protection Act","d":"K.S.A. 60-5320, enacted 2016 (L. 2016, ch. 58, eff. July 1, 2016). It protects the exercise of free speech (a communication in connection with a public issue or issue of public interest, defined to include health or safety, environmental, economic or community well-being, government, public officials or figures, and marketplace goods or services), the right to petition (broadly defined, including communications in or about judicial, official, legislative, and executive proceedings), and the right of association. It is to be construed liberally.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(a)-(c), (k)","v":true},{"t":"Motion to strike: 60 days; hearing within 30 days","d":"A party may move to strike a claim (including a counterclaim or cross-claim) that is based on, relates to, or responds to its protected activity. The motion may be filed within 60 days of service of the most recent complaint or later in the court’s discretion. A hearing must be held not more than 30 days after service of the motion.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(c)(1), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Burden shifting","d":"The movant must make a prima facie showing that the claim concerns its exercise of speech, petition, or association. The burden then shifts to the responding party to establish a likelihood of prevailing by presenting substantial competent evidence of a prima facie case; if it does, the motion is denied. The court considers the pleadings and supporting and opposing affidavits. A determination that the responding party is likely to prevail is not admissible later and does not change the burden of proof. Unlike some states, there is no separate step letting the movant defeat the claim by proving a defense.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(d)","v":true},{"t":"Discovery and proceedings stayed","d":"Filing the motion stays all discovery, motions, and other pending hearings until the ruling, except specified and limited discovery the court allows for good cause.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(e)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory fees and sanctions","d":"If the movant prevails, the court must award costs and reasonable attorney fees, without regard to state-law limits, plus additional relief, including sanctions on the responding party and its attorneys and law firms, needed to deter repetition. If the motion is frivolous or solely for delay, the court must award the responding party its fees and costs on the motion. A ruling against a government contractor must be sent to the head of the contracting government entity.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(g), (j)","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: 14 days; mandamus if no ruling","d":"The movant may file an interlocutory appeal from an order denying the motion if the notice of appeal is filed within 14 days after entry, or petition for mandamus if the trial court fails to rule in an expedited fashion. Trial-court proceedings are stayed pending the appeal.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(f)","v":true},{"t":"Exemptions","d":"Does not apply to enforcement actions brought by the Attorney General or a district or county attorney in the name of the State or a subdivision; claims against a person primarily selling or leasing goods or services over statements or conduct arising from a sale, lease, insurance product, or commercial transaction aimed at actual or potential customers (but this exemption does not reach claims based on dramatic, literary, musical, political, or artistic works, or newspaper or magazine articles); or claims under the Kansas insurance code or an insurance contract.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(h)-(i)","v":true},{"t":"Check case law before relying on the statute","d":"The Revisor’s annotations list one appellate decision, holding that review of a ruling on the motion is de novo (Doe v. Kan. State Univ., 61 Kan. App. 2d 128 (2021)), and a 2024 law review article on federal collateral-order review of anti-SLAPP rulings. Later Kansas appellate decisions on the act’s scope or constitutionality, and whether federal courts in Kansas apply it, were not researched.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320 (Revisor annotations)","v":false}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: Supreme Court Rule 116 (text not collected)","d":"Admission of out-of-state lawyers for a single case is governed by a Kansas Supreme Court rule (identified in the research brief as Rule 116). Its requirements, including any association with Kansas counsel, fees, and limits, could not be verified. The statute K.S.A. 7-103 gives the Supreme Court power to make rules for admission and discipline; the former statute on out-of-state attorneys, K.S.A. 7-104, is listed as repealed.","c":"K.S.A. 7-103; Kan. Sup. Ct. R. 116 (not collected). Kansas Supreme Court Rules not collected: kscourts.gov served a Cloudflare bot check on November 10, 2026","v":false},{"t":"Signature block must include e-mail and fax","d":"Every pleading, motion, and paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record and state the signer’s address, e-mail address, telephone number, and fax number; discovery papers need address, e-mail, and telephone. Unsigned papers are stricken unless promptly corrected.","c":"K.S.A. 60-211(a); 60-226(f)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Electronic filing and service are rule-driven","d":"District and appellate clerks must accept electronic filings to the extent provided by Supreme Court rule; electronic signatures and verifications that comply with the rules satisfy the code. A petition received electronically in compliance with Supreme Court rules counts as filed for commencement. Electronic service is allowed when authorized by Supreme Court or local rule. The e-filing rules themselves (who must e-file, registration for out-of-state counsel) were not collected.","c":"K.S.A. 60-271; 60-203(d); 60-205(b)(2)(F), (d)(3)","v":true},{"t":"Midnight deadline only for electronic or fax filing","d":"For electronic or fax filing, the last day ends at midnight in the court’s time zone; for other filing, when the clerk’s office is scheduled to close. Three extra days are added only after service by mail or by leaving the paper with the clerk, not after electronic or fax service.","c":"K.S.A. 60-206(a)(4), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation","d":"No limited-scope appearance provision appears in chapter 60, article 2. Any Supreme Court rule on limited representation was not collected and should be checked.","c":"Kansas Supreme Court Rules not collected: kscourts.gov served a Cloudflare bot check on November 10, 2026","v":false},{"t":"Out-of-state subpoenas for Kansas discovery","d":"Kansas has adopted the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act. Present the foreign subpoena to the clerk of the district court in the county where discovery will occur and pay the docket fee; the request is not an appearance. Motions to quash or enforce go to that county’s court.","c":"K.S.A. 60-228a","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Kansas Rules of Professional Conduct (Rule 226)","d":"Kansas lawyer conduct is governed by the Kansas Rules of Professional Conduct adopted by the Supreme Court (identified in the research brief as Supreme Court Rule 226), under the Court’s rulemaking power over admission and discipline. The rule text was not collected, so no differences from the ABA Model Rules are stated here.","c":"K.S.A. 7-103; Kan. Sup. Ct. R. 226 (not collected). Kansas Supreme Court Rules not collected: kscourts.gov served a Cloudflare bot check on November 10, 2026","v":false},{"t":"Post-verdict juror contact is regulated by statute","d":"After a civil jury is discharged, parties, lawyers, and their representatives may discuss deliberations or the verdict with a juror immediately afterward only with the juror’s consent. A later contact must first tell the juror the case, the party represented, the subject, the juror’s absolute right not to talk, and the right to see any declaration filed. Unreasonable nonconsensual contact must be reported to the court, and violations are punishable as contempt.","c":"K.S.A. 60-272","v":true},{"t":"Rule 11 analog has no safe harbor","d":"Sanctions for unwarranted pleadings, motions, or papers may be imposed after notice and an opportunity to respond, on the attorney, the law firm, or the party, with no 21-day withdrawal period like Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c)(2). A sanctions motion must be filed no later than 14 days after entry of judgment. The State and its agencies are covered.","c":"K.S.A. 60-211(c), (e)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory conferral certifications","d":"Motions to compel, motions for protective orders, and motions for sanctions for failing to respond to discovery must certify good-faith conferral and describe the steps taken by all attorneys or unrepresented parties to resolve the dispute.","c":"K.S.A. 60-226(c)(1); 60-237(a)(1), (d)(1)(B)","v":true},{"t":"Litigation funding disclosure","d":"Since July 1, 2025, a party that has a third-party litigation funding agreement must submit it to the court for in camera review, and deliver to all other parties a sworn statement about the funder, its control or approval rights, its access to confidential materials, its relationships with the opposing side or the court, the nature of its interest, and any funding from a foreign person of a country of concern, within 30 days after the action starts or the agreement is signed, whichever is later. Agreements that give the funder nothing beyond repayment plus interest of no more than 11.1% are excluded.","c":"K.S.A. 60-226(b)(3)(B), (g)","v":true}],"style":"State of Kansas","home":"https://www.kscourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Filing alone may not stop the limitations clock","d":"An action counts as commenced on the filing date only if service (or first publication) is made within 90 days after filing, extendable once by 30 days for good cause. Otherwise it is commenced only on the date of service. Relation back for a new defendant also depends on notice within the limitations period plus that service window.","c":"K.S.A. 60-203(a)-(b); 60-215(c)(3)"},{"t":"Caption and damages pleading rules","d":"The petition must state below the caption that it is filed under chapter 60 (“Petition Pursuant to K.S.A. Chapter 60”). Except in contract cases, a demand over $75,000 must say only that damages exceed $75,000, and a demand of $75,000 or less must state the specific amount. Before taking a default on an “exceeds $75,000” petition, notify the defendant of the amount by return-receipt delivery at least 14 days before judgment is sought.","c":"K.S.A. 60-207(c); 60-208(a)(2); 60-254(c)"},{"t":"Jury demand within 14 days or it is waived","d":"Serve a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue, and file it. A missing or unfiled demand waives jury trial, though the court may relieve an inadvertent waiver.","c":"K.S.A. 60-238(b), (d)"},{"t":"No initial disclosures, but expert and funding disclosures are mandatory","d":"Kansas has no Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1) initial disclosures. Expert disclosures (identity, subject, substance of facts and opinions, and for retained experts a summary of grounds) are due at least 90 days before trial absent an order, and are filed with the court. Litigation funding agreements must be submitted in camera and disclosed by sworn statement within 30 days after the case starts or the agreement is signed.","c":"K.S.A. 60-226(b)(3)(B), (b)(6)"},{"t":"Nonparty deposition timing tied to the case management conference","d":"Without stipulation or leave, a nonparty may not be deposed before the case management conference (which must be held within 45 days after an answer, unless extended) unless the notice certifies with facts that the deponent will leave Kansas. If no conference is held within 45 days after the answer, the restriction lapses.","c":"K.S.A. 60-216(b)(2); 60-230(a)(2)(A)(ii)"},{"t":"Document subpoenas need advance notice to all parties","d":"A subpoena for documents or inspection that is not tied to a deposition may be served only after notice is served on every party. A records-only subpoena under 60-245a requires 14 days’ advance notice with a copy of the proposed subpoena, and any party objection blocks issuance without a court order.","c":"K.S.A. 60-245(b); 60-245a(b)(1)(A)"},{"t":"Change-of-judge window is 7 days","d":"In prejudgment matters, a motion for change of judge must be filed within 7 days after pretrial or after written notice of the assigned judge, whichever is later, and each party gets only one change. The motion itself must not state grounds; an affidavit with grounds follows immediately if the judge refuses.","c":"K.S.A. 20-311d(a); 20-311f"},{"t":"Post-trial motions: 28 days, no extensions","d":"Renewed motions for judgment as a matter of law, motions to amend findings, new-trial motions, and motions to alter or amend must be filed within 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court has no power to extend those deadlines.","c":"K.S.A. 60-206(b)(2); 60-250(b); 60-252(b); 60-259(b), (f)"},{"t":"The appeal clock starts at the filed journal entry, not the oral ruling","d":"A judgment is not effective until a journal entry or judgment form is signed and filed; the 30-day notice of appeal runs from that entry, and the notice is filed in the district court. The only statutory extension is up to 30 days for excusable neglect based on not learning of the entry.","c":"K.S.A. 60-258; 60-2103(a)"},{"t":"Cross-appeal in 21 days","d":"An appellee who wants any ruling reviewed must file a notice of cross-appeal within 21 days after the notice of appeal was served on it and filed.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(h)"},{"t":"Object to Court of Appeals jurisdiction within 30 days","d":"A motion to transfer a case from the Court of Appeals to the Supreme Court must be filed within 30 days after the notice of appeal is served on the appellee; failing to file waives any objection to Court of Appeals jurisdiction.","c":"K.S.A. 20-3017"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP motion: 60 days, and fees are mandatory","d":"The motion to strike may be filed within 60 days of service of the most recent complaint (later only in the court’s discretion), and a hearing must occur within 30 days after service of the motion. A prevailing movant must be awarded fees and costs, and sanctions may reach the opposing lawyers and law firms. An appeal from denial requires notice within 14 days.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(d), (f), (g)"}],"judge":[{"t":"Change of judge: motion first, then affidavit","d":"A party or attorney who believes the assigned judge cannot give a fair trial files a motion for change of judge that must not state its grounds; the judge hears it informally. If the judge declines to step aside, the party must immediately file an affidavit alleging a statutory ground: the judge was counsel in the case, is otherwise interested, is related to a party, is a material witness, or has personal bias, prejudice, or interest (stating the facts and reasons). The chief judge, or another district judge, decides legal sufficiency; if sufficient, the case is reassigned. Prior rulings on legal issues are not a sufficient basis for bias. No one may be held in contempt for filing the affidavit.","c":"K.S.A. 20-311d; 20-311e","v":true},{"t":"One change per party; 7-day window","d":"No party gets more than one change of judge in an action. In prejudgment matters, the motion must be filed within 7 days after pretrial or after written notice of the judge assigned to hear the case, whichever is later; in post-judgment proceedings it may be filed at any time. This is a for-cause procedure, not a peremptory strike.","c":"K.S.A. 20-311f","v":true},{"t":"Automatic disqualification for close relatives","d":"In any contested case, a district judge is disqualified if any party or attorney of record is the judge’s spouse, parent, child, grandchild, grandparent, brother, or sister, or the spouse of one of those relatives.","c":"K.S.A. 20-311","v":true},{"t":"90-day reporting on undecided matters","d":"If a bench-tried case, motion, or objection is not decided within 90 days after final submission, the judge must file a written report with the Supreme Court explaining why.","c":"K.S.A. 60-252a","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Statutory, not constitutional, protections shown","d":"The collected texts support the Public Speech Protection Act (K.S.A. 60-5320) and a libel pleading rule: in a libel or slander action it suffices to allege generally that defamatory matter was published concerning the plaintiff, and the defendant may plead both truth and mitigating circumstances. No Kansas Bill of Rights text or case law on free speech was collected, so no claim is made that Kansas protects speech more broadly than the First Amendment.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320; 60-209(j)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Civil procedure is statutory","d":"Kansas’s rules of civil procedure are article 2 of the Code of Civil Procedure, enacted and amended by the Legislature, restyled to follow the federal rules in 2010 (L. 2010, ch. 135). Expect statutory changes each session, such as the 2025 litigation-funding disclosure. Local rules adopted by a majority of a district’s judges must be consistent with the code.","c":"K.S.A. 60-101, 60-201, 60-267","v":true},{"t":"Modified comparative fault and several liability","d":"A claimant recovers only if its negligence is less than that of the parties it claims against, with damages reduced proportionally; liability among defendants is several, by percentage of fault, determined by special verdict. On a defendant’s motion, others whose fault allegedly contributed must be joined, and crossclaims among co-parties arising from the occurrence are compulsory.","c":"K.S.A. 60-258a; 60-213(f)","v":true},{"t":"Ten of twelve jurors decide","d":"With a 12-member civil jury, agreement of 10 jurors suffices for a verdict; smaller juries must be unanimous unless the parties stipulate otherwise. Each side has 3 peremptory challenges.","c":"K.S.A. 60-247(c)(2); 60-248(a), (g)","v":true},{"t":"Nonparty business-records subpoenas","d":"Kansas has a records-only subpoena procedure: notice and a copy of the proposed subpoena go to all parties at least 14 days before issuance, a party objection stops issuance absent a court order, and the custodian may comply by mailing certified copies with a declaration within the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days.","c":"K.S.A. 60-245a","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Kansas statutes from the Office of Revisor of Statutes (ksrevisor.gov), fetched November 10, 2026: K.S.A. 60-2101 through 60-2107 (appellate procedure), K.S.A. 60-206, 60-223(f), 60-250, 60-252, 60-258, 60-259, 60-262 (post-judgment and stays), K.S.A. 60-5320(f) (anti-SLAPP appeals), K.S.A. 20-3001, 20-3002, 20-3016 through 20-3018, 20-3021 (Court of Appeals, transfer, review, docket fee), and Kan. Const. art. 3, §§ 1-3. The Kansas Supreme Court’s appellate practice rules (the Rule 2, 5, 6, 7, and 8 series on docketing, motions, briefs, rehearing, and petitions for review) were NOT collected: kscourts.gov served a Cloudflare “Attention Required” bot check, and per the collection rules it was not retried through a browser. Briefing deadlines, word limits, rehearing timing, and e-filing mechanics therefore are not stated as verified.","intro":{"t":"Kansas appeals: 30 days from a filed journal entry, and most civil appeals go to the Court of Appeals first","d":"Civil appeals from the district court go to the Kansas Court of Appeals (14 judges) unless a statute requires a direct appeal to the Supreme Court, chiefly when a district court has held a Kansas or federal statute unconstitutional. The Supreme Court reviews Court of Appeals decisions mainly by discretionary petition for review, and it can also take cases by transfer.\nThe notice of appeal is filed with the clerk of the district court within 30 days after entry of judgment, and a judgment is not entered until a journal entry or judgment form is signed by the judge and filed. Timely post-trial motions under 60-250(b), 60-252(b), and 60-259 restart the full 30 days from the order deciding them; those motions are due 28 days after judgment and the district court cannot extend them.\nThe procedural details after the notice of appeal (docketing, briefs, length limits, rehearing, petitions for review) are set by Kansas Supreme Court rules that could not be collected here. Treat every rule-based deadline in practice materials as needing a check against the current rules on kscourts.gov.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2101, 60-2102, 60-2103(a), 60-258; 60-206(b)(2); 20-3018"},"groups":[["Which court hears the appeal",[{"t":"Court of Appeals is the default","d":"The Court of Appeals hears appeals from district courts except cases reviewable in the district court and cases where a direct Supreme Court appeal is required by law, plus agency appeals where a statute sends them directly there. It may correct, modify, vacate, or reverse any district-court act, order, or judgment to assure it is just, legal, and free of abuse. It has 14 judges.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2101(a); 20-3001; 20-3002(a)"},{"t":"Direct appeal to the Supreme Court","d":"An appeal from a final civil judgment holding a Kansas or federal statute unconstitutional goes directly to the Supreme Court. Appeals of right also go there from a decision holding a statute unconstitutional under article 6 (education/school finance) of the Kansas Constitution, which must be filed within 30 days of the decision, and from final decisions in actions challenging or arising from the Kansas expanded lottery act and its gaming facility contracts.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2101(b); 60-2102(b)"},{"t":"Filed in the wrong court? It is transferred, not dismissed","d":"No case is dismissed solely because it was docketed in the wrong appellate court; the Supreme Court transfers it to the court with jurisdiction and it is treated as timely filed there.","c":"K.S.A. 20-3018(a)"},{"t":"Appeals from non-lawyer magistrate judges go to a district judge","d":"Orders and final decisions of a district magistrate judge who is not regularly admitted to practice law in Kansas are appealed to a district judge, by notice of appeal filed with the district court clerk within 14 days after entry; the notice must say the appeal is from a magistrate judge’s order. Such orders cannot be appealed directly to the Court of Appeals.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103a(a); 60-2102(a)"},{"t":"Appeals from local-government quasi-judicial orders","d":"If no other method is provided by law, a party aggrieved by a judicial or quasi-judicial order of a political or taxing subdivision appeals to the district court by filing a notice of appeal with the subdivision or agency within 30 days of entry, then filing true copies of the proceedings with the district court clerk and paying a docket fee.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2101(d)"}]],["What can be appealed",[{"t":"Final decisions, with review of every earlier ruling","d":"An appeal of right lies from a final decision in any action. In an appeal or cross-appeal from a final decision, any act or ruling from the beginning of the proceedings is reviewable, and a ruling made more than 30 days before the notice of appeal can still be reviewed.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2102(a)(4); 60-2103(i)"},{"t":"Interlocutory orders appealable as of right","d":"Kansas allows appeals of right from orders that discharge, vacate, or modify a provisional remedy; that grant, continue, modify, refuse, or dissolve an injunction; that grant or refuse mandamus, quo warranto, or habeas corpus; that appoint a receiver or refuse to wind up a receivership; and (unusually broad) any order involving the tax or revenue laws, title to real estate, the Kansas Constitution, or the federal constitution, laws, or treaties.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2102(a)(1)-(3)"},{"t":"Certified interlocutory appeals","d":"For any other civil order, the district judge (or a lawyer-magistrate) may state in writing in the order that it involves a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion and that an immediate appeal may materially advance the litigation. The Court of Appeals may then permit the appeal if application is made within 14 days after entry of the order. The application does not stay district-court proceedings unless a judge orders.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2102(c)"},{"t":"Class certification orders","d":"The Court of Appeals may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying class certification if application is made within 14 days after entry; no automatic stay.","c":"K.S.A. 60-223(f)"},{"t":"Partial judgments","d":"An order resolving fewer than all claims or parties is final only if the district court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and directs entry of judgment.","c":"K.S.A. 60-254(b)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP denials","d":"A movant whose motion to strike under the Public Speech Protection Act is denied may take an interlocutory appeal if the notice of appeal is filed within 14 days after entry of the order, or may seek mandamus if the court fails to rule in an expedited fashion. Further trial-court proceedings are stayed pending the appeal.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(f)"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"When judgment is entered","d":"No judgment is effective until a journal entry or judgment form is signed by the judge and filed with the clerk. The 30-day appeal clock runs from that entry. When judgment is by judgment form, the clerk must serve it on counsel within 3 business days, but failure of service does not affect validity.","c":"K.S.A. 60-258; 60-2103(a)"},{"t":"Notice of appeal: 30 days, filed in the district court","d":"File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court within 30 days from entry of judgment. The notice must name the appealing parties, designate the judgment or part appealed from, and name the appellate court. Serve it on all parties under 60-205; failure to serve does not invalidate the appeal. Later missteps do not void the appeal but can lead to sanctions including dismissal.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(a)-(b)"},{"t":"Narrow 30-day extension","d":"The district court may extend the appeal time by up to 30 days after the original deadline only for excusable neglect based on a party’s failure to learn of the entry of judgment. No other ground is listed in the statute.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(a)"},{"t":"Docket fee","d":"A party filing an appeal with the Court of Appeals or Supreme Court pays a $145 fee to the Clerk of the Supreme Court; a poverty affidavit may be filed instead, and the State and Kansas municipalities are exempt. The statute’s authority for an extra charge of up to $10 ran only through June 30, 2025.","c":"K.S.A. 20-3021"},{"t":"Record and docketing","d":"The record on appeal is filed and docketed at the time, and in the form, prescribed by Supreme Court rule; security for costs is also set by Supreme Court rule. Those rules were not collected.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(c), (g); 60-2104"},{"t":"Voluntary dismissal before docketing","d":"Before the record is filed in the appellate court, the parties may dismiss by stipulation filed in the district court with its approval, or the district court may dismiss on the appellant’s motion and notice.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(a)"}]],["Post-trial motions and tolling",[{"t":"Which motions restart the clock","d":"A timely renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law (60-250(b)), motion to amend or add findings (60-252(b)), motion to alter or amend (60-259(f)), or motion for new trial (60-259) terminates the running of the appeal time, and the full 30 days runs anew from the order granting or denying it (for a new-trial motion, from the order denying it). A 60-260 motion for relief from judgment is not on the list and does not affect finality.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(a); 60-260(c)(2)"},{"t":"28 days, no extensions","d":"Each of those motions must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court may not extend those deadlines. Opposing affidavits on a new-trial motion are due 14 days after service.","c":"K.S.A. 60-250(b); 60-252(b); 60-259(b), (d), (f); 60-206(b)(2)"}]],["Cross-appeals",[{"t":"21 days after the notice of appeal","d":"An appellee who wants review of rulings it complains of must give notice of cross-appeal within 21 days after the notice of appeal has been served on it and filed with the trial court clerk.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(h)"}]],["Stays and supersedeas bonds",[{"t":"14-day automatic stay; then bond","d":"No execution may issue until 14 days after entry of judgment. Injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed even on appeal unless the court orders. Otherwise an appellant gets a stay by a supersedeas bond approved by the district court; the stay takes effect on approval. The State and its agencies need not post bond.","c":"K.S.A. 60-262(a), (d), (e)"},{"t":"Bond amount and caps","d":"The bond is normally the full judgment plus costs, interest, and delay damages. On proof of undue hardship or denial of the right to appeal, the court may reduce it to $1,000,000 plus 25% of the excess over $1,000,000 (judgments of $1,000,000 or less stay at full amount). Undue hardship is presumed for a small business (2 to 50 employees, unaffiliated) facing a judgment over $2,500,000 arising from its ordinary business. No bond may exceed $25,000,000, but the caps fall away if the appellee proves the appellant is dissipating assets to avoid payment.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(d)"},{"t":"Provisional remedies and injunctions","d":"A party aggrieved by an order discharging, vacating, or modifying a provisional remedy, or modifying or dissolving an injunction, may have it suspended for up to 14 days on condition that it files a notice of appeal and obtains approval of a supersedeas bond within that period.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(d)(1)"},{"t":"Late or insufficient bond","d":"Before docketing in the appellate court, the district court may set a time to file a bond; after docketing, leave must be sought from the appellate court. A surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction and may be held liable on motion.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(e)-(f)"}]],["Transfer to the Supreme Court and review",[{"t":"Motion to transfer: 30 days, or the objection is waived","d":"Within 30 days after the notice of appeal is served on the appellee, any party may move in the Court of Appeals to transfer the case to the Supreme Court, alleging that an issue is outside the Court of Appeals’ jurisdiction, the case has significant public interest, it involves legal questions of major public significance, or caseload requires transfer. Failing to file the motion waives any objection to the Court of Appeals’ jurisdiction. The Court of Appeals may also request transfer, and the Supreme Court may transfer any case on its own motion.","c":"K.S.A. 20-3016; 20-3017; 20-3018(c)"},{"t":"Petition for review: 30 days after the decision","d":"A party aggrieved by a Court of Appeals decision may petition the Supreme Court for review within 30 days after the date of the decision. A rehearing motion in the Court of Appeals is allowed under Supreme Court rules but is not a prerequisite to review. Review is discretionary; factors include general importance, conflict with Supreme Court or other Court of Appeals decisions, the need for supervisory authority, and whether the ruling is final or interlocutory.","c":"K.S.A. 20-3018(b)"},{"t":"Appeal of right from the Court of Appeals","d":"A party may appeal to the Supreme Court as of right when a question under the U.S. or Kansas Constitution arises for the first time as a result of the Court of Appeals’ decision.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2101(b)"},{"t":"Opinions and mandate","d":"Appellate judges must file the opinion within 60 days after the decision; a memorandum opinion may be used where no new question of law is decided, while formal opinions carry a syllabus of the points of law. The mandate issues when the decision becomes final under Supreme Court rule and becomes part of the district court judgment. Appellate courts disregard technical errors that did not prejudice substantial rights. Costs are taxed as the appellate court directs.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2105; 60-2106; 60-2107"},{"t":"Supreme Court decisions need four justices","d":"The Supreme Court has at least seven justices; every case must be heard by at least four, and a decision requires a majority of those sitting and at least four votes.","c":"Kan. Const. art. 3, § 2"}]],["Not collected: verify in the Kansas Supreme Court Rules",[{"t":"Briefs, word limits, rehearing, docketing statements, e-filing","d":"Briefing schedules, length and word limits, the time for a Court of Appeals or Supreme Court rehearing motion, docketing requirements, the contents of a petition for review, oral argument, and appellate e-filing are set by Kansas Supreme Court rules. The rule texts could not be collected because kscourts.gov served a Cloudflare bot check to automated requests on November 10, 2026. No deadline, page limit, or word limit is stated here for those items.","c":"Kansas Supreme Court Rules (appellate practice series beginning at Rule 2.01); not collected"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil, district court to appellate court)","d":"30 days from entry of judgment (journal entry or judgment form signed and filed).\nFiled with the clerk of the district court. Extendable by up to 30 days only for excusable neglect based on failure to learn of the entry of judgment.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(a); 60-258","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Filed with the clerk of the district court. Extendable by up to 30 days only for excusable neglect based on failure to learn of the entry of judgment."}},{"t":"Appeal after a timely post-trial motion","d":"30 days from entry of the order granting or denying a 60-250(b), 60-252(b), or 60-259 motion (for a new-trial motion, the order denying it).\nFull 30 days runs anew.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order granting or denying a 60-250(b), 60-252(b), or 60-259 motion)","note":"Full 30 days runs anew."}},{"t":"Renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law","d":"28 days from entry of judgment (or jury discharge if the issue was not decided by verdict).\nCannot be extended (60-206(b)(2)).","c":"K.S.A. 60-250(b)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended (60-206(b)(2))."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial; motion to alter or amend","d":"28 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended. The court may order a new trial on its own within the same 28 days.","c":"K.S.A. 60-259(b), (f)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended. The court may order a new trial on its own within the same 28 days."}},{"t":"Motion to amend or add findings","d":"28 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended.","c":"K.S.A. 60-252(b)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended."}},{"t":"Notice of cross-appeal","d":"21 days from service and filing of the notice of appeal.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(h)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (service and filing of the notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Application for permissive interlocutory appeal","d":"14 days from entry of the order containing the district judge’s written certification.\nFiled with the Court of Appeals; procedure set by Supreme Court rule (not collected).","c":"K.S.A. 60-2102(c)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order containing the district judge’s written certification)","note":"Filed with the Court of Appeals; procedure set by Supreme Court rule (not collected)."}},{"t":"Application to appeal class-certification order","d":"14 days from entry of the order.\nNo automatic stay.","c":"K.S.A. 60-223(f)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"No automatic stay."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion to strike","d":"14 days from entry of the order denying the motion.\nTrial-court proceedings are stayed pending the appeal.","c":"K.S.A. 60-5320(f)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order denying the motion)","note":"Trial-court proceedings are stayed pending the appeal."}},{"t":"Appeal from a non-lawyer district magistrate judge","d":"14 days from entry of the order or decision.\nHeard by a district judge, not the Court of Appeals.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103a(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order or decision)","note":"Heard by a district judge, not the Court of Appeals."}},{"t":"Motion to transfer from Court of Appeals to Supreme Court","d":"30 days from service of the notice of appeal on the appellee.\nNot filing waives any objection to Court of Appeals jurisdiction.","c":"K.S.A. 20-3017","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of appeal on the appellee)","note":"Not filing waives any objection to Court of Appeals jurisdiction."}},{"t":"Petition for review in the Supreme Court","d":"30 days from date of the Court of Appeals decision.\nA rehearing motion is not a prerequisite. Whether a rehearing motion affects this time is governed by Supreme Court rule (not collected).","c":"K.S.A. 20-3018(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date of the Court of Appeals decision)","note":"A rehearing motion is not a prerequisite. Whether a rehearing motion affects this time is governed by Supreme Court rule (not collected)."}},{"t":"Appeal to Supreme Court from decision holding a statute unconstitutional under Kan. Const. art. 6","d":"30 days from filing of the preliminary or final decision.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2102(b)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the preliminary or final decision)","note":""}},{"t":"Appeal of local-government quasi-judicial order to district court","d":"30 days from entry of the order.\nOnly if no other appeal method is provided by law.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2101(d)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Only if no other appeal method is provided by law."}},{"t":"Suspension of order dissolving an injunction or provisional remedy","d":"14 days from order suspending it.\nNotice of appeal and approved supersedeas bond must be filed within the 14 days.","c":"K.S.A. 60-2103(d)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (order suspending it)","note":"Notice of appeal and approved supersedeas bond must be filed within the 14 days."}},{"t":"Automatic stay of execution","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nNot for injunctions or receiverships.","c":"K.S.A. 60-262(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Not for injunctions or receiverships."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal (civil, district court to appellate court)",30,"Entry of judgment (journal entry or judgment form signed and filed)","K.S.A. 60-2103(a); 60-258","Filed with the clerk of the district court. Extendable by up to 30 days only for excusable neglect based on failure to learn of the entry of judgment."],["Appeal after a timely post-trial motion",30,"Entry of the order granting or denying a 60-250(b), 60-252(b), or 60-259 motion (for a new-trial motion, the order denying it)","K.S.A. 60-2103(a)","Full 30 days runs anew."],["Renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law",28,"Entry of judgment (or jury discharge if the issue was not decided by verdict)","K.S.A. 60-250(b)","Cannot be extended (60-206(b)(2))."],["Motion for new trial; motion to alter or amend",28,"Entry of judgment","K.S.A. 60-259(b), (f)","Cannot be extended. The court may order a new trial on its own within the same 28 days."],["Motion to amend or add findings",28,"Entry of judgment","K.S.A. 60-252(b)","Cannot be extended."],["Notice of cross-appeal",21,"Service and filing of the notice of appeal","K.S.A. 60-2103(h)",""],["Application for permissive interlocutory appeal",14,"Entry of the order containing the district judge’s written certification","K.S.A. 60-2102(c)","Filed with the Court of Appeals; procedure set by Supreme Court rule (not collected)."],["Application to appeal class-certification order",14,"Entry of the order","K.S.A. 60-223(f)","No automatic stay."],["Notice of appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion to strike",14,"Entry of the order denying the motion","K.S.A. 60-5320(f)","Trial-court proceedings are stayed pending the appeal."],["Appeal from a non-lawyer district magistrate judge",14,"Entry of the order or decision","K.S.A. 60-2103a(a)","Heard by a district judge, not the Court of Appeals."],["Motion to transfer from Court of Appeals to Supreme Court",30,"Service of the notice of appeal on the appellee","K.S.A. 20-3017","Not filing waives any objection to Court of Appeals jurisdiction."],["Petition for review in the Supreme Court",30,"Date of the Court of Appeals decision","K.S.A. 20-3018(b)","A rehearing motion is not a prerequisite. Whether a rehearing motion affects this time is governed by Supreme Court rule (not collected)."],["Appeal to Supreme Court from decision holding a statute unconstitutional under Kan. Const. art. 6",30,"Filing of the preliminary or final decision","K.S.A. 60-2102(b)(1)",""],["Appeal of local-government quasi-judicial order to district court",30,"Entry of the order","K.S.A. 60-2101(d)","Only if no other appeal method is provided by law."],["Suspension of order dissolving an injunction or provisional remedy",14,"Order suspending it","K.S.A. 60-2103(d)(1)","Notice of appeal and approved supersedeas bond must be filed within the 14 days."],["Automatic stay of execution",14,"Entry of judgment","K.S.A. 60-262(a)","Not for injunctions or receiverships."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.kscourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Kansas Code of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"60-201","t":"Rules of civil procedure; citation; scope","s":"Article 2 of the Kansas Code of Civil Procedure may be cited as the “rules of civil procedure” and governs all civil actions and proceedings in the district courts, except cases under the Code of Civil Procedure for Limited Actions (chapter 61). Unlike the federal rules, these are statutes enacted by the Legislature; K.S.A. 60-102 directs that the code be liberally construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-202","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-203","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action is commenced on filing the petition only if service is obtained (or first publication is made) within 90 days after filing, extendable once by 30 days for good cause; otherwise it is commenced only on the date of service or first publication, which matters for the statute of limitations. If service is later held invalid for an irregularity, the filing date still counts if valid service follows within 90 days (plus up to 30 for good cause) of that ruling. An entry of appearance has the same effect as service; electronic filing that complies with Supreme Court rules counts as filing.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-204","t":"Process, generally","s":"The article 3 service methods are sufficient but do not displace other methods provided by law. Substantial compliance with a service method is valid service if the court finds the party served was made aware that an action that might affect the party was pending in a specified court.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-205","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Later pleadings, written motions, discovery papers required to be served, and notices, appearances, demands, and offers of judgment must be served on every party (not on a party in default for failure to appear, unless a new claim is asserted against it, which requires summons-type service). Service on a represented party goes to the attorney; it may be by hand, leaving at office or home, mail (complete on mailing), leaving with the clerk if no known address, fax (complete on machine confirmation), or electronic means authorized by Supreme Court or local rule. Papers are filed with a certificate of service within a reasonable time; depositions (other than under 60-227), interrogatories, document requests, and requests for admission are not filed until used, and only a certificate of service is filed for expert disclosures. Fax service is a Kansas addition not in Fed. R. Civ. P. 5.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-206","t":"Time, computation and extension","s":"Time is computed like Fed. R. Civ. P. 6: exclude the trigger day, count every day, and roll a weekend or legal holiday (including a day the Kansas Supreme Court orders observed) to the next business day; the last day ends at midnight for electronic or fax filing and at clerk’s-office closing otherwise. The court may extend time for good cause before expiration, or after expiration for excusable neglect, but may not extend the times under 60-250(b), 60-252(b), 60-259(b), (e), (f), and 60-260(b). A written motion and notice of hearing must be served at least 7 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits at least 1 day before. Three days are added after service by mail or by leaving with the clerk (not after electronic or fax service). The Chief Justice may extend or suspend deadlines in an emergency under K.S.A. 20-172.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-207","t":"Pleadings allowed; motions; form","s":"Allowed pleadings are the petition (Kansas’s name for the complaint), answer, answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim, third-party petition and answer, and a reply only if the court orders one. A motion must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state its grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought. Kansas-specific: the petition must state immediately below the caption that it is filed under chapter 60, e.g., “Petition Pursuant to K.S.A. Chapter 60.” A lost or withheld pleading may be replaced by a copy.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-208","t":"General rules of pleadings","s":"Notice pleading as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 8: a short and plain statement of the claim and a demand for relief. Kansas-specific damages rule: except in contract actions, a pleading seeking more than $75,000 in money damages must state only that the amount exceeds $75,000, while a claim for $75,000 or less must state the specific amount. Denials, the effect of failing to deny, and a list of 17 affirmative defenses (including comparative fault and injury by fellow servant) follow the federal pattern; alternative and inconsistent pleading is allowed.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-209","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity need not be alleged, but must be challenged by specific denial. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; conditions of mind generally; conditions precedent generally (denials with particularity). Special damages must be specifically stated; an amended petition adding punitive damages under K.S.A. 60-3703 states only whether damages exceed $75,000. Kansas additions: a claim on a written instrument may be pleaded by identifying it, reciting it, or attaching it; money tendered need not be deposited before trial; and in libel or slander it suffices to allege generally that defamatory matter was published concerning the plaintiff, and if not denied it need not be proved.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-210","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading needs a caption with the court’s name, a title, file number, and designation; the petition names all parties. Claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs, separate counts where that promotes clarity, and exhibits are part of the pleading. A party’s name change after filing may be noted of record by filing a certified record or an affidavit or declaration, and the changed name is then used in the alternative.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-211","t":"Signing; representations; sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or the unrepresented party) and state the signer’s address, e-mail address, telephone number, and fax number; unsigned papers are stricken unless promptly corrected. The certifications mirror Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(b). Key difference: Kansas has no 21-day safe harbor; after notice and an opportunity to respond, the court may sanction the attorney, firm, or party, and a sanctions motion may be filed any time during the case but no later than 14 days after entry of judgment. The State and its agencies are covered; sanctions against an inmate may be paid from the inmate’s account.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-212","t":"Defenses and objections","s":"Answer within 21 days after service of summons and petition (or within the time in the publication notice, at least 41 days after first publication); answer a counterclaim or crossclaim within 21 days; a reply when ordered within 21 days. A motion under this section moves the deadline to 14 days after notice of the court’s ruling (or after service of a more definite statement). The seven Rule 12(b) defenses, judgment on the pleadings, conversion to summary judgment, more definite statement (comply within 14 days), motion to strike (within 21 days if no response is allowed), joining motions, and waiver of personal jurisdiction, venue, process, and service defenses track Fed. R. Civ. P. 12. Kansas addition: a guardian or conservator answering for a minor or incapacitated person, or the attorney for a prisoner, must deny all prejudicial material allegations.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-213","t":"Counterclaims and cross-claims","s":"Compulsory and permissive counterclaims follow Fed. R. Civ. P. 13. Kansas differences: a claim connected to the opposing claim survives assignment, death, or the running of limitations, but only as an offset up to the opposing party’s award; and in a comparative-fault case under K.S.A. 60-258a, a crossclaim against a co-party arising from the same occurrence is compulsory. When a limited action is transferred or a district magistrate judge’s case is appealed, compulsory counterclaims and crossclaims must be pleaded within 21 days after service of the transfer order or notice of appeal.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-214","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may serve a third-party petition on a nonparty who is or may be liable to it; leave of court is needed if filed more than 14 days after serving the original answer. The third-party defendant’s defenses and claims track Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (and compulsory crossclaims under 60-213(f) apply). Kansas addition: a third-party plaintiff may execute on a judgment against the third-party defendant only to the extent it has actually paid the judgment against it.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-215","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"Amend once as a matter of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or, if a response is required, within 21 days after service of the responsive pleading or of a motion under 60-212(b), (e), or (f), whichever is earlier; otherwise with written consent or leave, freely given. Respond to an amended pleading within the time left on the original or 21 days, whichever is later. Amendments at and after trial, relation back (including the party-change rule measured by the 60-203 service period), and supplemental pleadings track Fed. R. Civ. P. 15.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-216","t":"Pretrial conferences; case management conference","s":"On any party’s request the court must (or on its own may) hold pretrial conferences and a case management conference, which must be held within 45 days after an answer is filed unless extended; the conference covers issues, ADR, ESI, privilege agreements (including K.S.A. 60-426a), a discovery plan and deadline, and dates for motions, joinder, amendment, and trial. Kansas-specific: if a case management conference is held, depositions of nonparties wait until after it (absent agreement, order, or 60-230(a)(2)(B)), but that restriction lapses if no conference is held within 45 days after the answer. A motion for more discovery time must be filed before the original period expires and include a discovery plan. The final pretrial order follows Supreme Court rule and may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice; sanctions and mandatory fees track Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(f).","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-217","t":"Parties; capacity","s":"Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest (with the usual list of representatives who may sue in their own names), and dismissal for not doing so waits a reasonable time for ratification, joinder, or substitution. Kansas additions: a claim that accrued under another state’s law may be sued on by whoever may sue on it there, and a Kansas-appointed administrator or executor may bring a foreign wrongful-death claim if that state’s law allows a personal representative to sue. Minors and incapacitated persons sue through a guardian, conservator, next friend, or guardian ad litem; public officers may be named by title. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 17(b), there is no separate capacity-to-sue provision here.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-218","t":"Joinder of claims; contingent claims","s":"A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, including contingent claims, such as a money claim with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance. Kansas addition: a plaintiff may plead a claim to set aside a release, settlement, or discharge of the original claim as fraudulent or wrongfully procured.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-219","t":"Required joinder of parties","s":"Required-party joinder and the equity-and-good-conscience factors when joinder is not feasible track Fed. R. Civ. P. 19, and a pleader must name unjoined required persons and explain why. Kansas addition: in an action affecting title to or a security interest in real property, a nominee of record for a beneficial owner (for example, a mortgage-registry nominee) who is subject to service must be joined.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-220","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials or other protective orders. Same as Fed. R. Civ. P. 20.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-221","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties on just terms at any time and sever any claim.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-222","t":"Interpleader","s":"A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join claimants as defendants and require them to interplead, and a defendant may interplead by crossclaim or counterclaim. Kansas addition (disclaiming interpleader): a party holding money or property claimed by another may say so in its answer, deposit or deliver it as ordered, and be discharged; the court may order the claimant (served like a summons) to appear and assert or relinquish the claim, and may bar a claimant who does not appear.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-223","t":"Class actions","s":"Prerequisites, the three types of class actions, certification, notice, judgment, subclasses, settlement approval, and class counsel and fees track Fed. R. Civ. P. 23 (in its pre-2018 form, without the newer settlement-notice and objector-payment provisions). Appeal of an order granting or denying certification is by permission of the Kansas Court of Appeals on application within 14 days after entry; it does not stay district-court proceedings unless the district judge or Court of Appeals orders.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-223a","t":"Derivative actions","s":"A shareholder or member derivative petition must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership and no collusion, and state with particularity the demand made (or why none was made). The plaintiff must fairly and adequately represent similarly situated holders; settlement or dismissal needs court approval and notice as the court orders.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-223b","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"An action by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class through named representatives may be maintained only if they will fairly and adequately protect the association and members; the 60-223(d) and (e) procedures apply.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-224","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right and permissive intervention on timely motion track Fed. R. Civ. P. 24. Kansas additions: when a Kansas statute, ordinance, regulation, or constitutional provision is challenged in a case in which the State or subdivision is not a party, the court may notify the chief legal officer and permit intervention, and must permit the Attorney General to intervene on proper application when notice is required by K.S.A. 75-764. The motion must be accompanied by a pleading setting out the claim or defense.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-225","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"After a party’s death, the court must on motion order substitution if the claim survives; if no motion is made within a reasonable time after service of a statement noting the death, the action by or against the decedent must be dismissed. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 25(a)(1), there is no fixed 90-day period. Incapacity, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers follow the federal pattern; the attorney may continue to act in the original party’s name until substitution.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-226","t":"Discovery","s":"Scope is relevance and proportionality, as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(1). Major differences: Kansas has no initial-disclosure requirement like Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1) and no Rule 26(f) conference. Expert disclosure must identify the expert, the subject matter, and the substance of facts and opinions, and for retained experts only a summary of the grounds for each opinion (not a full signed report), at least 90 days before trial (rebuttal within 30 days after the other side’s disclosure), filed with the court; expert supplementation is due 30 days before trial. New in 2025 (L. 2025, ch. 60): a party must submit any third-party litigation funding agreement to the court for in camera review, and serve all parties a sworn statement describing the funder, control rights, confidentiality access, relationships, the interest, and any funding from a foreign country of concern, within 30 days after the action begins or the agreement is signed, whichever is later. Protective-order motions require a good-faith conferral certification describing the steps taken.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-227","t":"Perpetuation of testimony","s":"Before suit, a verified petition may be filed in the county where an expected adverse party resides (for a will contest, the testator’s county of residence), served at least 21 days before the hearing (personally, by restricted mail, or as the court orders). If granted, the deposition may be used later against a party or someone with a similar interest and motive, generally only if the deponent is unavailable. Depositions may also be taken while an appeal is pending or may still be taken. Depositions taken under a similar procedure elsewhere are admissible to the same extent.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-228","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In Kansas, a deposition must be taken before a person authorized to administer oaths and a Kansas Supreme Court certified court reporter. Outside Kansas, before an officer authorized there or a person the court appoints; a court may issue a commission. Foreign-country depositions may proceed by treaty, letter of request, notice, or commission. The officer may not be a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or counsel, or financially interested.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-228a","t":"Uniform interstate depositions and discovery act","s":"To use an out-of-state subpoena in Kansas, submit the foreign subpoena to the clerk of the district court in the county where discovery will occur and pay the docket fee under K.S.A. 60-2001; the clerk issues a Kansas subpoena, opens a civil case number, and the request is not an appearance. The subpoena must list all counsel and unrepresented parties, is served under K.S.A. 60-303, and is governed by K.S.A. 60-245; motions to quash, modify, enforce, or for protection go to that county’s court.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-229","t":"Discovery procedure; stipulations","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate to deposition logistics and modify discovery procedures, but a stipulation extending discovery time needs court approval if it would interfere with the discovery-completion, motion-hearing, or trial dates.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-230","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"Any person may be deposed without leave except a second deposition, a prisoner, or (absent stipulation) a nonparty before the 60-216(b) case management conference unless the notice certifies with facts that the deponent will leave Kansas. Reasonable written notice is required; video may be added on notice; remote depositions by stipulation or order are deemed taken where the deponent answers. Objections must be concise and nonargumentative, with instructions not to answer only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limit, or move to terminate. The deponent has 30 days after notice of availability to review and sign changes. Differences from Fed. R. Civ. P. 30: no 10-deposition limit and no 1-day-of-7-hours limit appear in the text; the officer sends the sealed original to the attorney who arranged the transcript, who must store it; and only listed persons (officer, reporter, parties, attorneys and their paralegals, deponent) may attend unless stipulated or ordered.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-231","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"Any person may be deposed by written questions on notice served on all parties (leave needed in the same situations as 60-230). Cross-questions are due 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions, redirect 14 days after cross, and recross 14 days after redirect; the court may change these times. The officer takes, certifies, and sends the deposition, and the noticing party notifies others of completion.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-232","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"Depositions may be used against a party present, represented, or noticed, to the extent admissible as live testimony: to impeach, any deposition of an adverse party or its officer or designee for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or outside Kansas (unless procured by the offering party), unable to attend because of age, illness, infirmity, or imprisonment, beyond subpoena, or in exceptional circumstances. Kansas difference: “outside this state” alone qualifies, unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 32(a)(4)(B). A deposition taken early under 60-230(a)(2)(A)(ii) cannot be used against a party who could not get counsel despite diligence. Waiver-of-objection rules follow Fed. R. Civ. P. 32(d); objections to the form of a written recross-question are due within 7 days.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-233","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of process. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of process; timing may be changed by stipulation or order. Objections must be specific or are waived; contention interrogatories are allowed; the business-records option applies. Kansas difference: the statute sets no numerical limit (Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 caps at 25); check local rules and case management orders.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-234","t":"Production of documents, ESI, tangible things, and entry onto land","s":"Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of process. A written response is due in 30 days, or for a defendant 45 days after service of process. Objections must be specific, state whether material is being withheld, and allow inspection of the rest; ESI form rules and production rules track Fed. R. Civ. P. 34. Nonparties are reached by subpoena under 60-245 and 60-245a.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-235","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"On motion for good cause and notice, the court may order a party whose physical or mental condition (including blood group) is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner; the order sets time, place, scope, and the examiner, and the moving party advances expenses. The examined party may obtain the detailed report and reports of earlier exams, and must then provide its own like reports; the court may exclude an examiner’s testimony if a report is withheld. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 35(b)(4), the text does not say that requesting the report waives privilege.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-236","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of process. A matter is admitted unless a signed answer or objection is served within 30 days after service, or for a defendant 45 days after service of process. Answer, objection, sufficiency motion, and withdrawal rules track Fed. R. Civ. P. 36; admissions bind only in the pending action. No numerical limit appears in the text.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-237","t":"Compelling discovery; sanctions; failure to preserve ESI","s":"A motion to compel requires a certification of good-faith conferral that describes the steps taken by all counsel or unrepresented parties; motions about nonparties go to the district where discovery is taken. Fee shifting, sanctions for disobeying orders (established facts, preclusion, striking, stay, dismissal, default, contempt), exclusion of undisclosed experts and supplementation, failure-to-admit costs, and sanctions for not attending one’s own deposition or not responding at all track Fed. R. Civ. P. 37. Lost-ESI sanctions mirror Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(e): curative measures on prejudice; adverse presumption, instruction, dismissal, or default only on a finding of intent to deprive.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-238","t":"Right of trial by jury; demand; waiver","s":"The jury right under section 5 of the Kansas Bill of Rights or a statute is preserved. Serve a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served, and file it; other parties have 14 days after a partial demand to demand jury on other issues. Failure to serve and file waives jury trial, but the court may set aside a waiver in the interest of justice or when it was inadvertent; a demand may be withdrawn only with all parties’ consent.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-239","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"A demanded jury case is docketed as such and tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or the court finds no jury right under the Kansas constitution or statutes. Issues not properly demanded are tried to the court, which may still order a jury on motion; advisory and consent juries are allowed (consent verdicts bind, except in actions against the State where a statute provides for nonjury trial).","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-240","t":"Scheduling cases for trial; continuances","s":"Each district court schedules trials by rule, giving priority to cases entitled to it by law. Continuances are for good cause; one for absent evidence is at the requesting party’s cost unless ordered otherwise. Kansas-specific: a continuance for an absent witness or evidence must be supported by an affidavit or declaration stating the witness, the expected testimony and its basis, and efforts to procure it; the court may deny the motion if the opponent admits the witness would so testify and agrees the affidavit may be read at trial.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-241","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served, or by stipulation signed by all parties who appeared; a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal at plaintiff’s request is by court order. Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or comply operates on the merits unless the order says otherwise (except for jurisdiction, venue, or joinder). Kansas-specific: the court may on its own dismiss without prejudice for lack of prosecution only after at least 14 days’ notice to counsel of record. Costs of a previously dismissed action may be ordered and the new case stayed.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-242","t":"Multicounty and multidistrict litigation","s":"The court may consolidate or jointly try actions sharing a common question pending in counties of the same judicial district, and order separate trials (in the home county or another county of the district) while preserving jury rights. For related actions in different judicial districts, the Kansas Supreme Court, on request of a party or court, may transfer them to one county for consolidated discovery, pretrial proceedings, and possible trial before a designated judge; a joint trial requires all parties’ consent, and nonconsenting actions are returned for trial.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-243","t":"Testimony of witnesses; evidence","s":"Trial testimony is taken in open court; remote contemporaneous testimony is allowed for good cause in compelling circumstances with safeguards. Kansas keeps specific rules on leading and calling an adverse party or its officer, director, or managing agent as on cross; cross-examination is limited to the subject matter of direct. Offers of proof on excluded evidence are made outside the jury’s hearing (in bench trials, the court must take the evidence in full on request unless clearly inadmissible or privileged). Motions may be heard on affidavits, declarations, testimony, or depositions; interpreters are appointed under K.S.A. 75-4351 et seq.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-244","t":"Proof of records","s":"Authentication and admissibility of official records and other documents are governed by article 4 of chapter 60 (the Kansas rules of evidence), not by this section.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-245","t":"Subpoenas","s":"Subpoenas are issued by the clerk under seal (blank on request) or by a judge, may be served anywhere in Kansas under K.S.A. 60-303, and must tender one day’s witness fee and mileage if attendance is required. Kansas-specific: before serving a subpoena for documents or inspection that is not tied to a deposition, notice must first be served on every party. Written objections to production are due before the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days after service. The court must quash a subpoena that requires a nonparty to travel more than 100 miles from residence, employment, or regular business (for a nonresident, from where served), except to attend trial; a prisoner may be deposed only in the county of imprisonment. Contempt is punished under K.S.A. 20-1204.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-245a","t":"Subpoena of nonparty business records","s":"A Kansas procedure with no federal counterpart: a party may obtain copies of a nonparty’s business records by a records-only subpoena. At least 14 days before the subpoena issues, the party must serve all parties notice with a copy of the proposed subpoena; if any party objects before issuance, it issues only on court order. The custodian may comply within the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days by delivering true copies with a custodian’s declaration or affidavit (which the custodian files), may demand copying costs first, and may object in writing within the same period. Unused records may be destroyed or returned 30 days after the case ends. To require the custodian’s attendance or originals, use a 60-245 subpoena duces tecum.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-246","t":"Objections to rulings or orders","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action it wants or objects to and the grounds. A party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-247","t":"Jurors","s":"Enough prospective jurors are called to leave 12 after challenges. The parties or counsel are entitled to conduct voir dire, and the court decides all challenges for cause. Each side gets 3 peremptory challenges (multiple plaintiffs or defendants count as one party unless the court finds a good-faith controversy among them and allows more), exercised without revealing to the juror which party challenged.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-248","t":"Jury trial procedure","s":"Civil juries have 12 members unless the parties stipulate to fewer or to a stated majority verdict. Key difference from federal practice: with a 12-person jury, 10 jurors’ agreement is enough for a verdict; other juries must be unanimous unless the parties stipulate. The section also covers jury views, keeping jurors together, admonitions on separation, written questions from the jury, discharge, polling, and alternates (each party gets 1 peremptory challenge to alternates; alternates may be retained after submission and substituted).","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-248a","t":"(Repealed)","s":"Repealed by L. 2010, ch. 135, § 225.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-249","t":"Special verdict; general verdict; written questions","s":"The court may require a special verdict or a general verdict with written questions. An issue not submitted is waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires, and the court may then make its own finding. Inconsistent answers are handled as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 49.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-249a","t":"Itemized verdict, personal injury actions","s":"In a personal-injury damages case, the factfinder must itemize noneconomic damages (pain and suffering, disability, disfigurement and accompanying mental anguish), medical expenses, and other economic losses, and must split each between damages to date and future damages. Instructions and the verdict form may mention only items on which evidence was introduced. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-250","t":"Judgment as a matter of law; new trial","s":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law may be made any time before submission to the jury. A renewed motion (with an optional alternative request for new trial) must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, or 28 days after discharge if the issue was not decided by the verdict. Kansas addition: in comparative-fault cases, the court must reserve ruling on a motion by a party joined under K.S.A. 60-258a(c) until all evidence of that party’s fault is in. Conditional new-trial rulings follow Fed. R. Civ. P. 50; the 28-day deadline cannot be extended (60-206(b)(2)).","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-251","t":"Jury instructions; objections","s":"Requests are filed at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered. The court must disclose proposed instructions and give an opportunity to object on the record outside the jury’s hearing before argument, and must instruct at the close of evidence before argument (unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 51, which allows instructing after argument). Objections must state the matter and grounds distinctly; unpreserved instruction error is reviewed only if clearly erroneous and affecting substantial rights.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-252","t":"Findings and conclusions by the court","s":"In bench trials, advisory-jury trials, and (Kansas addition) when entering summary judgment, the court must find facts specially and state conclusions of law separately; also when granting or refusing an interlocutory injunction (except in divorce cases). Findings are reviewed for clear error. A motion to amend or add findings is due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment and cannot be extended. Judgment on partial findings follows Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(c).","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-252a","t":"Trial by the court; 90-day report","s":"If a judgment or decision is not entered within 90 days after trial and final submission of a bench-tried case, or after submission of a motion or objection, the judge must file a written report with the Supreme Court explaining why. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-252b","t":"Rules of supreme court","s":"Directs the Supreme Court to adopt rules and require reports from district courts or clerks to enforce the 90-day reporting requirement of K.S.A. 60-252a.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-253","t":"Trial by masters","s":"Reference to a master (including a referee, auditor, commissioner, or examiner) is by consent, or otherwise only if the ends of justice will be measurably advanced, and in jury cases only for complex or voluminous accounts. The master’s first meeting must be set within 21 days after the order of reference; in nonjury cases findings are accepted unless clearly erroneous and objections are due within 14 days after notice of filing. The master may enforce unpaid compensation by writ of execution. This keeps the older federal structure, not the 2003 version of Fed. R. Civ. P. 53.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-254","t":"Judgment","s":"A judgment is the final determination of the parties’ rights. Partial final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties requires an express determination of no just reason for delay; otherwise the order remains revisable. A default judgment may not exceed or differ from the demand. Kansas-specific: where the petition stated only that damages exceed $75,000, the party seeking default must notify the defaulting party of the amount by return-receipt delivery (or as ordered) at least 14 days before judgment is sought.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-255","t":"Default","s":"A party that fails to plead or otherwise defend is in default, and on request and showing of entitlement the court renders judgment (Kansas does not use a separate clerk’s entry of default). A party who has appeared must get written notice of the request at least 7 days before the hearing. Default against a minor or incapacitated person requires an appearing representative or guardian ad litem; against the State, its officers, or agencies only on satisfactory evidence. Set aside under 60-260(b) and 60-309.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-256","t":"Summary judgment","s":"Unless a local rule or order differs, a motion may be filed any time until 30 days after the close of all discovery; the response is due 21 days after service of the motion or the date a responsive pleading is due, whichever is later; a reply is due 14 days after the response. Judgment is rendered if there is no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Partial adjudication of established facts, affidavit and declaration requirements, the unavailable-facts continuance, and bad-faith affidavit sanctions follow the pre-2010 Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 wording. Kansas Supreme Court rules (Rule 141 is believed to govern summary-judgment statements of fact) were not collected; verify before filing.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-257","t":"Declaratory judgment","s":"Article 2 governs procedure for declaratory judgments under article 17 of chapter 60, with jury demands under 60-238 and 60-239. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-258","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"No judgment is effective until a journal entry or judgment form is signed by the judge and filed with the clerk. When judgment is entered by judgment form, the clerk must serve it on all attorneys of record within 3 days (excluding weekends and legal holidays); failure to serve does not affect validity. This filing date starts post-judgment and appeal deadlines.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-258a","t":"Comparative negligence","s":"Kansas modified comparative fault: a claimant recovers only if its negligence is less than that of the parties against whom it claims (so 50% bars recovery), with damages reduced by its percentage; a decedent’s negligence is imputed in wrongful-death claims. The jury (or court) must return special findings of each party’s percentage and total damages. On motion of a defendant, any other person whose causal negligence allegedly contributed must be joined. Liability is several only, in proportion to each party’s fault. Applies also in limited actions.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-258b","t":"(Repealed)","s":"Repealed by L. 2010, ch. 135, § 225.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-259","t":"New trial; motion to alter or amend judgment","s":"Grounds include abuse of discretion, misconduct, accident or surprise, lack of opportunity to be heard, erroneous rulings or instructions, passion or prejudice, a verdict contrary to the evidence, newly discovered evidence, and corruption (which requires a new trial as of right with costs charged to the party who procured the verdict). A new-trial motion, and a motion to alter or amend, must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court may act on its own within the same 28 days; none of these times can be extended. The motion must state specific reasons, not track the statute. Affidavits are filed with the motion; opposing affidavits within 14 days after service.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-260","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time, but after an appeal is docketed only with the appellate court’s leave. The six grounds for relief track Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b); the motion must be made within a reasonable time, and for mistake, newly discovered evidence, or fraud no more than one year after entry. The motion does not affect finality. Independent actions, relief under K.S.A. 60-309 for defendants served by publication without personal notice, and relief for fraud on the court remain available; old bills of review and coram nobis writs are abolished.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-261","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors that do not affect any party’s substantial rights must be disregarded at every stage.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-262","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce judgment","s":"No execution or enforcement for 14 days after entry of judgment (Fed. R. Civ. P. 62(a) now uses 30 days); injunctions and receivership judgments are not automatically stayed. The court may stay enforcement pending post-trial motions on security terms. On appeal, a stay is obtained by supersedeas bond, effective on court approval of the bond; the State, its officers, and agencies need not post bond. The court may modify injunctions pending appeal; appellate courts keep their own stay powers.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-263","t":"Disability of judge","s":"If the trial judge becomes unable to act after the verdict or after findings and conclusions are filed, another judge of the court may perform the remaining duties, or may grant a new trial if unable to perform them. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 63, the text does not provide for a successor judge to take over mid-trial by certifying familiarity with the record.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-264","t":"Enforcing orders for and against a nonparty","s":"An order granting relief for or enforceable against a nonparty is enforced by the same procedure as for a party.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-265","t":"Applicability of article","s":"Article 2 applies to civil actions in district court other than limited actions. Other district-court proceedings follow their own codes, but may borrow article 2 provisions when those codes are silent. When nothing in the article addresses a matter, the court must proceed in a just and equitable manner protecting all affected parties.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-266","t":"Jurisdiction and venue not affected","s":"Article 2 neither extends nor limits district-court jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-267","t":"Rules by district courts","s":"A majority of the judges of a district court may adopt local rules consistent with article 2, furnished to the Supreme Court on adoption. Where the article is silent, district courts may regulate practice consistently with it and Supreme Court rules.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-268","t":"(Repealed)","s":"Repealed by L. 2017, ch. 75, § 10.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-269","t":"(Repealed)","s":"Repealed by L. 2005, ch. 101, § 19.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-270","t":"Retaining original records until case closed","s":"Parties or attorneys holding original deposition transcripts, discovery responses, and other original discovery materials must keep them until the case is closed (order terminating the case filed and all appeals ended or time expired, or judgment satisfied or barred under K.S.A. 60-2403), then may dispose of them unless a protective order, rule, statute, or agreement requires otherwise.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-271","t":"Acceptance of filings by electronic means","s":"To the extent provided by Supreme Court rule, district and appellate court clerks must accept electronic filings of any document. Electronic signatures and verifications consistent with Supreme Court rules satisfy 60-211 and every other signing or verification requirement in the code. The Supreme Court’s electronic filing rules themselves were not collected (kscourts.gov blocked automated access).","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"},{"n":"60-272","t":"Contact with jurors after verdict","s":"Added in 2018 and outside 60-201 to 60-271, but codified in article 2. Before discharge, the judge must tell jurors they have an absolute right to discuss or not discuss deliberations. Parties and counsel may talk to a juror right after discharge only with consent; a later contact must first disclose the case, the party represented, the subject, the juror’s right to refuse, and the juror’s right to see any declaration filed. Unreasonable nonconsensual contact must be reported, and violations are punishable as contempt.","u":"https://ksrevisor.gov/statutes/ksa_ch60.html"}]});
